import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.6 (Day 1) — Combat Engine RPG Build: Attack &amp; Crit Resolver</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.3, 2.5 · 90-Minute RPG Build (Day 1 of 2).</strong> Your character
    has ability scores from Unit 2 and a <code>Dice</code> engine from Unit 1. Over the next two
    days you will build <code>Combat.java</code> — the tactical combat resolver that powers every
    dungeon fight in your RPG. Today you build Parts 1 and 2 (deterministic strike evaluation and
    live dice integration); next class (<code>3.6b</code>) you build damage mitigation, shield
    clamping, and tactical stance rounds.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download All Three into the Same Folder):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/Combat.java" download>Combat.java</a> — bare-minimum compiling stub
            for both days of the 3.6 build.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/CombatCheck.java" download>CombatCheck.java</a> — automated acceptance
            test suite (32 checks across Parts 1–5).
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a> — your dice engine from Unit 1.7 (or
            the reference copy if yours did not pass <code>DiceCheck</code>).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java Combat.java CombatCheck.java
java CombatCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>evaluateStrike(int d20Roll, int attackBonus, int targetAC, int baseDamage, boolean canCrit)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Professional game engines — and AP Computer Science A Free Response questions — separate
        <strong>random roll generation</strong> from <strong>rule evaluation</strong>. Why? Because
        if a method rolls a random <code>d20</code> inside itself, you have to run it 100 times
        just to hope a Natural 1 or Natural 20 shows up! By passing <code>d20Roll</code> and
        <code>baseDamage</code> as parameters into <code>evaluateStrike</code>, we can test every
        single edge case deterministically.
    </p>

    <h4>5e Strike Rules</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>When <code>canCrit</code> is <code>true</code> (Players &amp; Bosses):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>A <code>d20Roll</code> of <code>20</code> is a <strong>Critical Hit</strong>:
                    it automatically hits regardless of <code>attackBonus</code> or
                    <code>targetAC</code>, and returns <code>baseDamage * 2</code>.</li>
                <li>A <code>d20Roll</code> of <code>1</code> is a <strong>Critical Miss</strong>:
                    it automatically misses regardless of <code>attackBonus</code> or
                    <code>targetAC</code>, and returns <code>0</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Standard Attack (any other roll, OR whenever <code>canCrit</code> is <code>false</code>):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Compute the total attack roll: <code>d20Roll + attackBonus</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>"Meets it, beats it":</strong> If the total is greater than or equal to
                    <code>targetAC</code>, the strike hits and returns <code>baseDamage</code>.</li>
                <li>Otherwise, the strike misses and returns <code>0</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The Natural 1 Override Bug (Branch Order Matters!)</h3>
        <p>
            Suppose a Level 15 Paladin has <code>attackBonus = 18</code> and attacks a weak
            Goblin with <code>targetAC = 10</code>. The Paladin rolls a <strong>Natural 1</strong>
            on the d20 (<code>d20Roll = 1</code>).
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Broken branch order — trace what happens when d20Roll = 1, attackBonus = 18, targetAC = 10:
if (canCrit &amp;&amp; d20Roll == 20) {
    return baseDamage * 2;
} else if (d20Roll + attackBonus &gt;= targetAC) {
    return baseDamage;
} else if (canCrit &amp;&amp; d20Roll == 1) {
    return 0;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace the code above with <code>evaluateStrike(1, 18, 10, 8, true)</code>. Which
                branch executes, what value is returned, and why is the <code>d20Roll == 1</code>
                branch unreachable whenever <code>1 + attackBonus &gt;= targetAC</code>?</li>
            <li>Now trace <code>evaluateStrike(20, 2, 15, 6, false)</code> (a standard monster
                with <code>canCrit = false</code> rolling a 20 against AC 15). What must it return,
                and what would happen if your first branch only checked
                <code>if (d20Roll == 20)</code> without checking <code>canCrit</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Implement <code>evaluateStrike</code> in <code>Combat.java</code> and run
        <code>java CombatCheck</code> until all <strong>9 checks in Part 1</strong> report
        <code>PASS</code>.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · RPG Engine Integration</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>playerAttack(...)</code> &amp; <code>monsterAttack(...)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Now connect your deterministic <code>evaluateStrike</code> rule engine to your
        <code>Dice.java</code> utility class! Because <code>evaluateStrike</code> already knows all
        the rules for crits, fumbles, and Armor Class, <code>playerAttack</code> and
        <code>monsterAttack</code> should NOT duplicate that <code>if/else</code> chain — they
        should roll the dice and call <code>evaluateStrike</code>!
    </p>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>playerAttack</code> &amp; <code>monsterAttack</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signatures:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>public static int playerAttack(int attackBonus, int targetAC, int damageDie, String targetName)</code></li>
                <li><code>public static int monsterAttack(int attackBonus, int targetAC, int damageDie, String monsterName)</code></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Behavior for <code>playerAttack</code>:</strong>
            <ol>
                <li>Roll a 20-sided die using <code>Dice.roll(20)</code> and a damage die using
                    <code>Dice.roll(damageDie)</code>.</li>
                <li>Call <code>evaluateStrike(d20, attackBonus, targetAC, baseDmg, true)</code> to
                    compute the final damage dealt.</li>
                <li>Print a clear combat log line describing the outcome (crit, hit, or miss) against
                    <code>targetName</code>, and return the damage dealt.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Behavior for <code>monsterAttack</code>:</strong>
            <ol>
                <li>Roll a 20-sided die using <code>Dice.roll(20)</code> and a damage die using
                    <code>Dice.roll(damageDie)</code>.</li>
                <li>Call <code>evaluateStrike(d20, attackBonus, targetAC, baseDmg, false)</code>
                    (standard monsters do not crit or auto-miss on 1/20).</li>
                <li>Print a combat log line for <code>monsterName</code> and return the damage dealt.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Single Source of Truth</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is calling <code>evaluateStrike</code> from inside <code>playerAttack</code>
                and <code>monsterAttack</code> better software design than copy-pasting the
                <code>if/else</code> conditions into all three methods? If the game designer later
                changes crits to triple damage (<code>baseDamage * 3</code>), how many lines of code
                do you have to edit?</li>
            <li>Look at how <code>CombatCheck.java</code> tests <code>playerAttack</code> in Part 2:
                it calls <code>playerAttack(0, 50, 6, "Dragon")</code> 250 times. Since
                <code>20 + 0</code> is only <code>20</code> (way below AC 50), how is it possible
                for the player to ever deal damage in that test?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 1 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All <strong>14 checks in Parts 1 and 2</strong> of <code>java CombatCheck</code> report <code>PASS</code></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/combat-system.html">Student RPG Guide: Combat System</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/relational-operators.html">Relational Operators</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Logic</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
